/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 36-40
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:36, name:"The Scroll the King Burned", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 36:32",verse:"Then took Jeremiah another roll, and gave it to Baruch the scribe, the son of Neriah; who wrote therein from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of the book which Jehoiakim king of Judah had burned in the fire: and there were added besides unto them many like words."}, facts:["Did you know the scroll took a whole year to reach the public? It was written in Jehoiakim's fourth year but read aloud in the fifth year, in the ninth month, on a proclaimed fast (Jeremiah 36:1, 36:9).","Did you know three princes, Elnathan, Delaiah, and Gemariah, begged the king not to burn the roll, but he would not hear them (Jeremiah 36:25)?","Did you know the replacement scroll held everything the burned one had, plus more? God told Jeremiah to add 'many like words' (Jeremiah 36:32)."], questions:[
{q:"In what year of Jehoiakim's reign did this word come to Jeremiah?", options:["The fourth year","The fifth year","The ninth year","The eleventh year"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:1",verse:"And it came to pass in the fourth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, that this word came unto Jeremiah from the LORD, saying,", insight:"God dates His word precisely; He is not vague about when He speaks."},
{q:"What did God tell Jeremiah to take?", options:["A roll of a book","A tablet of stone","A sheet of papyrus","A bundle of reeds"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:2",verse:"Take thee a roll of a book, and write therein all the words that I have spoken unto thee against Israel, and against Judah, and against all the nations, from the day I spake unto thee, from the days of Josiah, even into this day.", insight:"The word of God was meant to be written down and kept, not left to memory alone."},
{q:"Against whom were all these words to be written?", options:["Against Israel, Judah, and all the nations","Against Egypt only","Against the priests of Jerusalem","Against the kings of Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:2",verse:"Take thee a roll of a book, and write therein all the words that I have spoken unto thee against Israel, and against Judah, and against all the nations, from the day I spake unto thee, from the days of Josiah, even into this day.", insight:"No one is outside God's accounting; His word speaks to nations as well as souls."},
{q:"What was God's hope in having the words written?", options:["That Judah would hear, turn from evil, and be forgiven","That Babylon would fear and retreat","That the priests would copy them for the temple","That the king would be praised in song"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:3",verse:"It may be that the house of Judah will hear all the evil which I purpose to do unto them; that they may return every man from his evil way; that I may forgive their iniquity and their sin.", insight:"'It may be' reveals a God who hopes for repentance, not one who rushes to punish."},
{q:"Whom did Jeremiah call to write the roll?", options:["Baruch the son of Neriah","Gemariah the son of Shaphan","Michaiah the son of Gemariah","Elishama the scribe"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:4",verse:"Then Jeremiah called Baruch the son of Neriah: and Baruch wrote from the mouth of Jeremiah all the words of the LORD, which he had spoken unto him, upon a roll of a book.", insight:"Baruch wrote at Jeremiah's mouth; faithful helpers carry the prophet's burden with their pens."},
{q:"Why could Jeremiah not read the roll in the LORD's house himself?", options:["He was shut up and could not go","He was sick in bed","He had fled to Egypt","He feared the princes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:5",verse:"And Jeremiah commanded Baruch, saying, I am shut up; I cannot go into the house of the LORD:", insight:"Shut up but not shut down; when Jeremiah could not go, he sent the word ahead of him."},
{q:"On what day was Baruch to read the roll in the house of the LORD?", options:["Upon the fasting day","On the sabbath","At the new moon","On the day of atonement"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:6",verse:"Therefore go thou, and read in the roll, which thou hast written from my mouth, the words of the LORD in the ears of the people in the LORD’S house upon the fasting day: and also thou shalt read them in the ears of all Judah that come out of their cities.", insight:"On the fasting day, when hearts were meant to be soft, the word was read aloud."},
{q:"In what year and month was the fast proclaimed?", options:["The fifth year of Jehoiakim, in the ninth month","The fourth year, in the seventh month","The ninth year, in the tenth month","The eleventh year, in the fourth month"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:9",verse:"And it came to pass in the fifth year of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, in the ninth month, that they proclaimed a fast before the LORD to all the people in Jerusalem, and to all the people that came from the cities of Judah unto Jerusalem.", insight:"A whole year passed between the writing and the reading; God's word waits for its appointed hour."},
{q:"Where did Baruch read the words to all the people?", options:["In the chamber of Gemariah, at the entry of the new gate of the LORD's house","In the king's winterhouse","In the court of the prison","In the house of Jonathan the scribe"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:10",verse:"Then read Baruch in the book the words of Jeremiah in the house of the LORD, in the chamber of Gemariah the son of Shaphan the scribe, in the higher court, at the entry of the new gate of the LORD’S house, in the ears of all the people.", insight:"The word was read in the open court, where all could hear; truth does not whisper."},
{q:"Whom did the princes send to bring Baruch to them?", options:["Jehudi the son of Nethaniah","Michaiah the son of Gemariah","Elnathan the son of Achbor","Zedekiah the son of Hananiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:14",verse:"Therefore all the princes sent Jehudi the son of Nethaniah, the son of Shelemiah, the son of Cushi, unto Baruch, saying, Take in thine hand the roll wherein thou hast read in the ears of the people, and come. So Baruch the son of Neriah took the roll in his hand, and came unto them.", insight:"The princes sent for Baruch politely, but the word they heard would soon reach a king."},
{q:"How did the princes react when they heard all the words?", options:["They were afraid, and said, We will surely tell the king","They laughed and tore the roll","They fasted and prayed","They crowned Baruch a prophet"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:16",verse:"Now it came to pass, when they had heard all the words, they were afraid both one and other, and said unto Baruch, We will surely tell the king of all these words.", insight:"Fear is the first fruit of hearing God's word; what they did with that fear is the tragedy."},
{q:"What did the princes ask Baruch?", options:["How didst thou write all these words at his mouth?","Who paid thee to write this?","Why readest thou on the fast day?","Where is Jeremiah hiding?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:17",verse:"And they asked Baruch, saying, Tell us now, How didst thou write all these words at his mouth?", insight:"They questioned the process because the message was too plain to argue with."},
{q:"How did Baruch answer them?", options:["He pronounced all these words unto me with his mouth, and I wrote them with ink in the book","I dreamed them in the night","I copied them from the priests","I found them in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:18",verse:"Then Baruch answered them, He pronounced all these words unto me with his mouth, and I wrote them with ink in the book.", insight:"Word for word, mouth to ink; Baruch's honest testimony still stands."},
{q:"What did the princes tell Baruch to do?", options:["Go and hide, thou and Jeremiah, and let no man know where ye be","Flee to Babylon","Burn the roll thyself","Deny the words before the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:19",verse:"Then said the princes unto Baruch, Go, hide thee, thou and Jeremiah; and let no man know where ye be.", insight:"They believed the word enough to protect the men who spoke it, but not enough to obey it."},
{q:"Where was the king sitting when the roll was read to him?", options:["In the winterhouse, with a fire on the hearth before him","In the temple court","In the gate of the city","In his chariot"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:22",verse:"Now the king sat in the winterhouse in the ninth month: and there was a fire on the hearth burning before him.", insight:"Warm by his winter fire, the king felt no chill from the words that should have frozen him with fear."},
{q:"What did the king do after Jehudi had read three or four leaves?", options:["He cut it with the penknife and cast it into the fire","He rent his garments and wept","He called the priests to interpret it","He sent it back to Jeremiah for correction"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:23",verse:"And it came to pass, that when Jehudi had read three or four leaves, he cut it with the penknife, and cast it into the fire that was on the hearth, until all the roll was consumed in the fire that was on the hearth.", insight:"Leaf by leaf into the flames; you can burn the paper, but the word keeps coming back."},
{q:"How did the king and his servants react to all these words?", options:["They were not afraid, nor rent their garments","They trembled and fasted","They released Baruch and Jeremiah","They asked for more of the roll"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:24",verse:"Yet they were not afraid, nor rent their garments, neither the king, nor any of his servants that heard all these words.", insight:"Unmoved hearts are the real tragedy here; the fire was in the hearth, but none in their conscience."},
{q:"Whom did the king command to seize Baruch and Jeremiah?", options:["Jerahmeel the son of Hammelech, Seraiah the son of Azriel, and Shelemiah the son of Abdeel","The Chaldean captains","The temple guard","The men of Benjamin"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:26",verse:"But the king commanded Jerahmeel the son of Hammelech, and Seraiah the son of Azriel, and Shelemiah the son of Abdeel, to take Baruch the scribe and Jeremiah the prophet: but the LORD hid them.", insight:"Three officials were sent, but the LORD hid them; God's messengers are never truly in man's power."},
{q:"What did God command Jeremiah after the king burned the roll?", options:["Take another roll and write in it all the former words","Flee to Egypt with Baruch","Speak no more to the king","Wait seven years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:28",verse:"Take thee again another roll, and write in it all the former words that were in the first roll, which Jehoiakim the king of Judah hath burned.", insight:"Burn one roll and God dictates another; His word is not finished when men are finished with it."},
{q:"What judgment did God pronounce on Jehoiakim?", options:["None of his line would sit on David's throne, and his body would lie exposed to heat by day and frost by night","He would live long in peace","He would be carried to Babylon in honor","His sons would rule forever"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 36:30",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD of Jehoiakim king of Judah; He shall have none to sit upon the throne of David: and his dead body shall be cast out in the day to the heat, and in the night to the frost.", insight:"The man who burned the roll would find no rest; contempt for God's word ends in contempt for the man."}
]},
{n:37, name:"The Imprisoned Prophet", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 37:17",verse:"Then Zedekiah the king sent, and took him out: and the king asked him secretly in his house, and said, Is there any word from the LORD? And Jeremiah said, There is: for, said he, thou shalt be delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah was arrested during a pause in the siege, when Pharaoh's army had briefly drawn the Chaldeans away from Jerusalem (Jeremiah 37:5, 37:11-13)?","Did you know Zedekiah moved Jeremiah from the dungeon to the court of the prison and ordered him a daily piece of bread from the bakers' street until the city's bread ran out (Jeremiah 37:21)?","Did you know the charge against Jeremiah was desertion, that he was 'falling away to the Chaldeans,' the very people he had spent his life warning Judah about (Jeremiah 37:13)?"], questions:[
{q:"Who reigned in Judah instead of Coniah the son of Jehoiakim?", options:["Zedekiah the son of Josiah","Jehoiakim the son of Josiah","Jehoiachin the son of Jehoiakim","Gedaliah the son of Ahikam"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:1",verse:"And king Zedekiah the son of Josiah reigned instead of Coniah the son of Jehoiakim, whom Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon made king in the land of Judah.", insight:"Nebuchadrezzar made him king, but only the LORD could make him a man of God."},
{q:"How did Zedekiah, his servants, and the people respond to God's words through Jeremiah?", options:["They did not hearken","They repented in sackcloth","They wrote them in a book","They sent them to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:2",verse:"But neither he, nor his servants, nor the people of the land, did hearken unto the words of the LORD, which he spake by the prophet Jeremiah.", insight:"A king, his court, and his people all closed their ears together; unbelief loves company."},
{q:"Whom did Zedekiah send to Jeremiah?", options:["Jehucal the son of Shelemiah and Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah","Jehudi and Elnathan","Irijah and Jonathan","Pashur and Malchiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:3",verse:"And Zedekiah the king sent Jehucal the son of Shelemiah and Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah the priest to the prophet Jeremiah, saying, Pray now unto the LORD our God for us.", insight:"The king who would not listen to God's word still wanted the prophet's prayers."},
{q:"What did the king's messengers ask Jeremiah to do?", options:["Pray now unto the LORD our God for us","Flee to Egypt","Curse the Chaldeans","Anoint a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:3",verse:"And Zedekiah the king sent Jehucal the son of Shelemiah and Zephaniah the son of Maaseiah the priest to the prophet Jeremiah, saying, Pray now unto the LORD our God for us.", insight:"'Pray for us' is right on the lips of men who will not obey; prayer is no substitute for hearing."},
{q:"What happened when Pharaoh's army came out of Egypt?", options:["The Chaldeans that besieged Jerusalem departed","Jerusalem surrendered at once","The Egyptians joined the siege","Zedekiah fled the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:5",verse:"Then Pharaoh’s army was come forth out of Egypt: and when the Chaldeans that besieged Jerusalem heard tidings of them, they departed from Jerusalem.", insight:"A temporary lifting of the siege; God lets men taste relief so they will not mistake it for rescue."},
{q:"What did God say Pharaoh's army would do?", options:["Return to Egypt into their own land","Besiege Jerusalem with the Chaldeans","Defeat Babylon in battle","Settle in the land of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:7",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel; Thus shall ye say to the king of Judah, that sent you unto me to enquire of me; Behold, Pharaoh’s army, which is come forth to help you, shall return to Egypt into their own land.", insight:"Egypt's help would melt away; do not lean on a reed that breaks."},
{q:"What did God say the Chaldeans would do?", options:["Come again, fight the city, take it, and burn it with fire","Make peace with Zedekiah","Withdraw to Babylon forever","Crown Jeremiah king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:8",verse:"And the Chaldeans shall come again, and fight against this city, and take it, and burn it with fire.", insight:"Plain and terrible; God's warnings are never vague when judgment is near."},
{q:"What warning did God give against self-deception?", options:["Deceive not yourselves, saying, The Chaldeans shall surely depart","Trust not in Egypt's horses","Fear not the king of Babylon","Hide not in the caves"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:9",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Deceive not yourselves, saying, The Chaldeans shall surely depart from us: for they shall not depart.", insight:"'Deceive not yourselves'; the most dangerous lies are the ones we tell ourselves."},
{q:"What if only wounded men were left of the Chaldean army?", options:["They would rise up every man in his tent and burn the city with fire","They would beg for mercy","They would join Israel","They would die of their wounds"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:10",verse:"For though ye had smitten the whole army of the Chaldeans that fight against you, and there remained but wounded men among them, yet should they rise up every man in his tent, and burn this city with fire.", insight:"Even wounded men in God's hand are enough; judgment does not need a full army."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah going when the siege lifted?", options:["Into the land of Benjamin, to separate himself among the people","To Egypt with Pharaoh's army","To Babylon to plead for Judah","To the wilderness to hide"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:12",verse:"Then Jeremiah went forth out of Jerusalem to go into the land of Benjamin, to separate himself thence in the midst of the people.", insight:"He went home to Benjamin in the lull; even prophets take ordinary journeys, and trouble finds them there."},
{q:"Who seized Jeremiah in the gate of Benjamin?", options:["Irijah the son of Shelemiah, a captain of the ward","Jonathan the scribe","Pashur the priest","Jehucal the prince"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:13",verse:"And when he was in the gate of Benjamin, a captain of the ward was there, whose name was Irijah, the son of Shelemiah, the son of Hananiah; and he took Jeremiah the prophet, saying, Thou fallest away to the Chaldeans.", insight:"A captain of the ward with a false charge; doing your duty badly can look like treason to small men."},
{q:"What did Irijah accuse Jeremiah of?", options:["Falling away to the Chaldeans","Stealing the temple gold","Plotting against the king","Worshipping idols"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:13",verse:"And when he was in the gate of Benjamin, a captain of the ward was there, whose name was Irijah, the son of Shelemiah, the son of Hananiah; and he took Jeremiah the prophet, saying, Thou fallest away to the Chaldeans.", insight:"The prophet who wept over Jerusalem was called a traitor; faithfulness is often misread."},
{q:"How did Jeremiah answer the charge?", options:["It is false; I fall not away to the Chaldeans","I confess my guilt","I appeal to Pharaoh","I will leave the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:14",verse:"Then said Jeremiah, It is false; I fall not away to the Chaldeans. But he hearkened not to him: so Irijah took Jeremiah, and brought him to the princes.", insight:"A short, plain denial; truth does not need a long defense."},
{q:"What did the princes do to Jeremiah?", options:["They smote him and put him in prison in the house of Jonathan the scribe","They released him with honor","They sent him to the king","They gave him bread daily"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:15",verse:"Wherefore the princes were wroth with Jeremiah, and smote him, and put him in prison in the house of Jonathan the scribe: for they had made that the prison.", insight:"The princes beat the messenger; anger at the message always lands on the man."},
{q:"Where did Jeremiah remain, and for how long?", options:["In the dungeon and the cabins, many days","In the king's palace, one night","In the temple, three days","In Egypt, a year"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:16",verse:"When Jeremiah was entered into the dungeon, and into the cabins, and Jeremiah had remained there many days;", insight:"Many days in the dungeon; God's servants are not exempt from dark, slow places."},
{q:"What did Zedekiah ask Jeremiah secretly in his house?", options:["Is there any word from the LORD?","Wilt thou pray for me?","Shall I flee to Egypt?","Wilt thou deny thy prophecy?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:17",verse:"Then Zedekiah the king sent, and took him out: and the king asked him secretly in his house, and said, Is there any word from the LORD? And Jeremiah said, There is: for, said he, thou shalt be delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon.", insight:"Secret questions reveal a troubled conscience; Zedekiah wanted a word, but not the Word."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah's answer?", options:["Thou shalt be delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon","The city shall be spared","Egypt shall save thee","Thou shalt die in peace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:17",verse:"Then Zedekiah the king sent, and took him out: and the king asked him secretly in his house, and said, Is there any word from the LORD? And Jeremiah said, There is: for, said he, thou shalt be delivered into the hand of the king of Babylon.", insight:"The same hard word, given in secret as in public; Jeremiah never edited God's message for his audience."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah ask the king?", options:["What have I offended against thee, or thy servants, or this people?","Why hast thou forsaken the LORD?","When wilt thou let me go?","Where are my accusers?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:18",verse:"Moreover Jeremiah said unto king Zedekiah, What have I offended against thee, or against thy servants, or against this people, that ye have put me in prison?", insight:"An innocent man's question; suffering does not always mean sinning."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah ask about the false prophets?", options:["Where are now your prophets which prophesied the king of Babylon shall not come?","Why do ye feed them at the king's table?","Who anointed them prophets?","When will they repent?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:19",verse:"Where are now your prophets which prophesied unto you, saying, The king of Babylon shall not come against you, nor against this land?", insight:"False prophets disappear when their prophecies do; truth is the only prophecy that shows up."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah beg of the king?", options:["Not to return him to the house of Jonathan the scribe, lest he die there","To set him free at once","To make him a priest","To send him to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 37:20",verse:"Therefore hear now, I pray thee, O my lord the king: let my supplication, I pray thee, be accepted before thee; that thou cause me not to return to the house of Jonathan the scribe, lest I die there.", insight:"He pleaded for his life plainly; courage and a cry for mercy can live in the same heart."}
]},
{n:38, name:"The Miry Dungeon", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 38:20",verse:"But Jeremiah said, They shall not deliver thee. Obey, I beseech thee, the voice of the LORD, which I speak unto thee: so it shall be well unto thee, and thy soul shall live."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah was lowered with cords into a cistern with no water, only mire, and Ebed-melech cushioned the cords with old rags so they would not cut his arms (Jeremiah 38:6, 38:11-12)?","Did you know the man who rescued Jeremiah was Ebed-melech the Ethiopian, a eunuch in the king's house, and God later promised to spare his life for his courage (Jeremiah 38:7, 39:17-18)?","Did you know Zedekiah confessed his real fear to Jeremiah? He dreaded the Jews who had defected, lest they mock him, and that fear kept him from obeying God (Jeremiah 38:19)."], questions:[
{q:"Which princes heard the words Jeremiah spoke to all the people?", options:["Shephatiah, Gedaliah, Jucal, and Pashur","Elnathan, Delaiah, Gemariah, and Zedekiah","Elishama, Shemaiah, Achbor, and Hananiah","Irijah, Zephaniah, Jehucal, and Seraiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:1",verse:"Then Shephatiah the son of Mattan, and Gedaliah the son of Pashur, and Jucal the son of Shelemiah, and Pashur the son of Malchiah, heard the words that Jeremiah had spoken unto all the people, saying,", insight:"Four princes heard and hated the message; the word that saves some enrages others."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say would happen to those who remained in the city?", options:["They would die by sword, famine, and pestilence","They would be carried to Egypt","They would see the Chaldeans retreat","They would be made princes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, He that remaineth in this city shall die by the sword, by the famine, and by the pestilence: but he that goeth forth to the Chaldeans shall live; for he shall have his life for a prey, and shall live.", insight:"Stay and die, or surrender and live; sometimes God's mercy wears the clothes of defeat."},
{q:"What did the princes ask the king to do with Jeremiah?", options:["Put him to death, for he weakened the hands of the men of war","Make him a captain","Send him to Babylon as an envoy","Crown him with honor"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:4",verse:"Therefore the princes said unto the king, We beseech thee, let this man be put to death: for thus he weakeneth the hands of the men of war that remain in this city, and the hands of all the people, in speaking such words unto them: for this man seeketh not the welfare of this people, but the hurt.", insight:"They called truth-telling treason; when men love war more than wisdom, the prophet looks like the enemy."},
{q:"What did Zedekiah answer the princes?", options:["Behold, he is in your hand: for the king is not he that can do any thing against you","I will protect the prophet","Take him to the temple","Let the people judge him"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:5",verse:"Then Zedekiah the king said, Behold, he is in your hand: for the king is not he that can do any thing against you.", insight:"A king who would not stand up; weakness in a ruler is cruelty to the righteous."},
{q:"Into what was Jeremiah cast?", options:["The dungeon of Malchiah, where there was no water but mire","A pit of snakes","The king's wine cellar","A dry cistern outside the wall"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:6",verse:"Then took they Jeremiah, and cast him into the dungeon of Malchiah the son of Hammelech, that was in the court of the prison: and they let down Jeremiah with cords. And in the dungeon there was no water, but mire: so Jeremiah sunk in the mire.", insight:"No water, only mire; they meant to let him sink and starve, out of sight of men but not of God."},
{q:"Who heard that Jeremiah had been put in the dungeon?", options:["Ebed-melech the Ethiopian, one of the eunuchs in the king's house","Gedaliah the governor","Baruch the scribe","Jonathan the scribe"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:7",verse:"Now when Ebed-melech the Ethiopian, one of the eunuchs which was in the king’s house, heard that they had put Jeremiah in the dungeon; the king then sitting in the gate of Benjamin;", insight:"An Ethiopian eunuch, a foreigner on the margins, saw what the princes of Judah would not."},
{q:"What did Ebed-melech tell the king?", options:["These men have done evil to Jeremiah, who is like to die for hunger, for there is no more bread in the city","The Chaldeans have breached the wall","The princes are plotting against thee","Jeremiah has escaped to Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:9",verse:"My lord the king, these men have done evil in all that they have done to Jeremiah the prophet, whom they have cast into the dungeon; and he is like to die for hunger in the place where he is: for there is no more bread in the city.", insight:"He spoke plainly to power at personal risk; courage often comes from unexpected quarters."},
{q:"How many men did the king give Ebed-melech for the rescue?", options:["Thirty","Three","Ten","Fifty"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:10",verse:"Then the king commanded Ebed-melech the Ethiopian, saying, Take from hence thirty men with thee, and take up Jeremiah the prophet out of the dungeon, before he die.", insight:"Thirty men to lift one prophet from the mire; heaven spares no expense on its servants."},
{q:"What did Ebed-melech take from under the treasury?", options:["Old cast clouts and old rotten rags","New linen garments","A ladder of brass","Sacks of bread"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:11",verse:"So Ebed-melech took the men with him, and went into the house of the king under the treasury, and took thence old cast clouts and old rotten rags, and let them down by cords into the dungeon to Jeremiah.", insight:"Old rags for a rescue; God uses worn, cast-off things to lift His wounded."},
{q:"Where did Ebed-melech tell Jeremiah to put the rags?", options:["Under his armholes, under the cords","Over his eyes","Around his feet","Across his mouth"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:12",verse:"And Ebed-melech the Ethiopian said unto Jeremiah, Put now these old cast clouts and rotten rags under thine armholes under the cords. And Jeremiah did so.", insight:"Under the armholes, where the cords would cut; mercy thinks of the details."},
{q:"Where did Zedekiah bring Jeremiah for a private meeting?", options:["Into the third entry in the house of the LORD","To the king's winterhouse","To the gate of Benjamin","To the dungeon door"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:14",verse:"Then Zedekiah the king sent, and took Jeremiah the prophet unto him into the third entry that is in the house of the LORD: and the king said unto Jeremiah, I will ask thee a thing; hide nothing from me.", insight:"The third entry, a secret chamber; the king wanted God's counsel without the court's eyes."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah say when the king promised to ask him a thing?", options:["If I declare it, wilt thou not surely put me to death? And if I counsel thee, wilt thou not hearken?","I will tell thee nothing","Speak, and I will obey","I am ready to die"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:15",verse:"Then Jeremiah said unto Zedekiah, If I declare it unto thee, wilt thou not surely put me to death? and if I give thee counsel, wilt thou not hearken unto me?", insight:"Honest fear, plainly spoken; Jeremiah knew his king's record."},
{q:"What did Zedekiah swear to Jeremiah?", options:["As the LORD liveth, I will not put thee to death nor give thee to these men","I will make thee my counselor","I will free all the prisoners","I will burn the city myself"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:16",verse:"So Zedekiah the king sware secretly unto Jeremiah, saying, As the LORD liveth, that made us this soul, I will not put thee to death, neither will I give thee into the hand of these men that seek thy life.", insight:"A secret oath from a wavering king; even weak men can keep one promise."},
{q:"What was the way of life God offered Zedekiah?", options:["Go forth to the king of Babylon's princes, and thy soul shall live, and the city shall not be burned","Flee to Egypt by night","Hide in the temple","Fight the Chaldeans to the end"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:17",verse:"Then said Jeremiah unto Zedekiah, Thus saith the LORD, the God of hosts, the God of Israel; If thou wilt assuredly go forth unto the king of Babylon’s princes, then thy soul shall live, and this city shall not be burned with fire; and thou shalt live, and thine house:", insight:"Surrender and live; God's hardest commands are sometimes His kindest."},
{q:"What if Zedekiah would not go forth?", options:["The city would be given to the Chaldeans, burned with fire, and he would not escape","He would be forgiven anyway","The Chaldeans would retreat","Egypt would rescue him"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:18",verse:"But if thou wilt not go forth to the king of Babylon’s princes, then shall this city be given into the hand of the Chaldeans, and they shall burn it with fire, and thou shalt not escape out of their hand.", insight:"No third option; with God, refusal is itself a choice with consequences."},
{q:"What was Zedekiah afraid of?", options:["The Jews that had fallen to the Chaldeans, lest they mock him","The king of Babylon's anger","His own princes","A famine in the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:19",verse:"And Zedekiah the king said unto Jeremiah, I am afraid of the Jews that are fallen to the Chaldeans, lest they deliver me into their hand, and they mock me.", insight:"He feared men's mockery more than God's judgment; the fear of man is a snare with a crown on it."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah promise if the king obeyed?", options:["They shall not deliver thee; it shall be well with thee, and thy soul shall live","Thou shalt rule forever","The Chaldeans shall serve thee","Thy sons shall be princes"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:20",verse:"But Jeremiah said, They shall not deliver thee. Obey, I beseech thee, the voice of the LORD, which I speak unto thee: so it shall be well unto thee, and thy soul shall live.", insight:"'Obey, I beseech thee'; the prophet pleads like a friend, not a judge."},
{q:"What taunt did Jeremiah say the palace women would sing?", options:["Thy friends have set thee on, and have prevailed against thee: thy feet are sunk in the mire","The king is mighty in battle","Blessed be the king forever","The Chaldeans are defeated"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:22",verse:"And, behold, all the women that are left in the king of Judah’s house shall be brought forth to the king of Babylon’s princes, and those women shall say, Thy friends have set thee on, and have prevailed against thee: thy feet are sunk in the mire, and they are turned away back.", insight:"His friends would fail him and his feet would sink; the very mire Jeremiah knew would become the king's song."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah told to say if the princes questioned him?", options:["I presented my supplication before the king, that he would not cause me to return to Jonathan's house","I told the king to surrender","I blessed the king's army","I prophesied nothing"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:26",verse:"Then thou shalt say unto them, I presented my supplication before the king, that he would not cause me to return to Jonathan’s house, to die there.", insight:"A true word that concealed a deeper one; wisdom sometimes speaks in part."},
{q:"How long did Jeremiah remain in the court of the prison?", options:["Until the day that Jerusalem was taken","Three days","Forty days","One year"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 38:28",verse:"So Jeremiah abode in the court of the prison until the day that Jerusalem was taken: and he was there when Jerusalem was taken.", insight:"He stayed until the city fell; the prophet outlasted the kingdom he warned."}
]},
{n:39, name:"Jerusalem Falls", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 39:7",verse:"Moreover he put out Zedekiah’s eyes, and bound him with chains, to carry him to Babylon."}, facts:["Did you know the siege lasted about eighteen months? It began in Zedekiah's ninth year, in the tenth month, and the city was broken up in his eleventh year, in the fourth month (Jeremiah 39:1-2).","Did you know Nebuchadrezzar himself ordered Jeremiah protected and honored, even letting the prophet dictate his own treatment (Jeremiah 39:11-12)?","Did you know the poorest people, who owned nothing, were left in Judah and given vineyards and fields on the spot, while the nobles were carried away (Jeremiah 39:10)?"], questions:[
{q:"When did Nebuchadrezzar besiege Jerusalem?", options:["In the ninth year of Zedekiah, in the tenth month","In the eleventh year, in the fourth month","In the fourth year of Jehoiakim","In the fifth year, in the ninth month"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:1",verse:"In the ninth year of Zedekiah king of Judah, in the tenth month, came Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon and all his army against Jerusalem, and they besieged it.", insight:"The siege began in winter; judgment often arrives in an ordinary season."},
{q:"When was the city broken up?", options:["In the eleventh year of Zedekiah, in the fourth month, on the ninth day","In the ninth year, in the tenth month","In the tenth year, on the first day","In the twelfth year, in the seventh month"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:2",verse:"And in the eleventh year of Zedekiah, in the fourth month, the ninth day of the month, the city was broken up.", insight:"A year and a half of siege, then the walls broke; God's patience has an ending."},
{q:"Which Babylonian princes sat in the middle gate?", options:["Nergal-sharezer, Samgar-nebo, Sarsechim, Rab-saris, and Nergal-sharezer Rabmag","Nebuzar-adan, Nebushasban, and Evil-merodach","The elders of Moab and Ammon","Pharaoh's captains and the men of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:3",verse:"And all the princes of the king of Babylon came in, and sat in the middle gate, even Nergal-sharezer, Samgar-nebo, Sarsechim, Rab-saris, Nergal-sharezer, Rabmag, with all the residue of the princes of the king of Babylon.", insight:"Foreign princes in the middle gate; the seat of judgment had changed hands."},
{q:"How did Zedekiah flee the city?", options:["By night, by the way of the king's garden, by the gate betwixt the two walls","In a chariot by day","Through the temple courts","Down the king's highway with his army"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:4",verse:"And it came to pass, that when Zedekiah the king of Judah saw them, and all the men of war, then they fled, and went forth out of the city by night, by the way of the king’s garden, by the gate betwixt the two walls: and he went out the way of the plain.", insight:"By night, by a garden path; kings who will not surrender must sneak away like thieves."},
{q:"Where was Zedekiah overtaken?", options:["In the plains of Jericho","At the gates of Jerusalem","In the wilderness of Judah","At the river Jordan"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:5",verse:"But the Chaldeans’ army pursued after them, and overtook Zedekiah in the plains of Jericho: and when they had taken him, they brought him up to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon to Riblah in the land of Hamath, where he gave judgment upon him.", insight:"Overtaken on the plain; no road runs far enough to outrun God's word."},
{q:"To what place was Zedekiah brought for judgment?", options:["To Riblah in the land of Hamath","To Babylon at once","To the middle gate","To the plains of Jericho"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:5",verse:"But the Chaldeans’ army pursued after them, and overtook Zedekiah in the plains of Jericho: and when they had taken him, they brought him up to Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon to Riblah in the land of Hamath, where he gave judgment upon him.", insight:"Riblah, far from home; judgment is often pronounced in a strange land."},
{q:"What did the king of Babylon do at Riblah?", options:["He slew the sons of Zedekiah before his eyes, and all the nobles of Judah","He pardoned Zedekiah's sons","He made Zedekiah his servant","He crowned a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:6",verse:"Then the king of Babylon slew the sons of Zedekiah in Riblah before his eyes: also the king of Babylon slew all the nobles of Judah.", insight:"Before his eyes, then darkness; the last sight of a disobedient king was his own ruin."},
{q:"What happened to Zedekiah?", options:["His eyes were put out, and he was bound with chains to be carried to Babylon","He was slain at Riblah","He escaped to Egypt","He was made governor of Judah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:7",verse:"Moreover he put out Zedekiah’s eyes, and bound him with chains, to carry him to Babylon.", insight:"Blinded and bound, exactly as Jeremiah had said; God's word keeps its appointments."},
{q:"What did the Chaldeans do to Jerusalem?", options:["They burned the king's house and the people's houses, and broke down the walls","They rebuilt the temple","They left the city untouched","They crowned Gedaliah in the gate"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:8",verse:"And the Chaldeans burned the king’s house, and the houses of the people, with fire, and brake down the walls of Jerusalem.", insight:"Fire and broken walls; the city that would not hear was left in ruins."},
{q:"Who carried the remnant captive to Babylon?", options:["Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard","Nebushasban Rab-saris","Nergal-sharezer Rabmag","The king of Babylon himself"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:9",verse:"Then Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard carried away captive into Babylon the remnant of the people that remained in the city, and those that fell away, that fell to him, with the rest of the people that remained.", insight:"The captain of the guard did the grim work; empires run on obedient men."},
{q:"Who was left in the land of Judah?", options:["The poor of the people, which had nothing, who were given vineyards and fields","The princes and the priests","The soldiers and their captains","No one at all"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:10",verse:"But Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard left of the poor of the people, which had nothing, in the land of Judah, and gave them vineyards and fields at the same time.", insight:"The poor inherited the land; God often lifts the lowest when He lays the highest low."},
{q:"What charge did Nebuchadrezzar give concerning Jeremiah?", options:["He gave charge to Nebuzar-adan concerning Jeremiah","He ordered Jeremiah slain","He ignored the prophet","He sent Jeremiah to Babylon in chains"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:11",verse:"Now Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon gave charge concerning Jeremiah to Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard, saying,", insight:"Even the conqueror knew the prophet's worth; God's servants are honored in strange places."},
{q:"What was the king's order about Jeremiah's treatment?", options:["Take him, look well to him, do him no harm, and do unto him even as he shall say","Bind him with the other captives","Leave him in the prison court","Send him to Riblah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:12",verse:"Take him, and look well to him, and do him no harm; but do unto him even as he shall say unto thee.", insight:"'Do unto him even as he shall say'; the prophet got to name his own treatment."},
{q:"Who was sent to release Jeremiah from the court of the prison?", options:["Nebuzar-adan, Nebushasban, and Nergal-sharezer, with all the princes","The Chaldean soldiers alone","The men of Judah","The priests of the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:13",verse:"So Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard sent, and Nebushasban, Rab-saris, and Nergal-sharezer, Rabmag, and all the king of Babylon’s princes;", insight:"A delegation of princes came for one prisoner; heaven's favor opens iron doors."},
{q:"To whom was Jeremiah committed?", options:["To Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan","To Baruch the scribe","To Ebed-melech","To the captain of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:14",verse:"Even they sent, and took Jeremiah out of the court of the prison, and committed him unto Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan, that he should carry him home: so he dwelt among the people.", insight:"Committed to Gedaliah, of the house of Shaphan; God placed Jeremiah with friends."},
{q:"When did God's word come to Jeremiah about Ebed-melech?", options:["While he was shut up in the court of the prison","After he reached Mizpah","In the middle gate","On the road to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:15",verse:"Now the word of the LORD came unto Jeremiah, while he was shut up in the court of the prison, saying,", insight:"God remembered the Ethiopian while Jeremiah was still a prisoner; no dungeon blocks God's promises."},
{q:"What would God bring upon the city?", options:["His words for evil, and not for good","Peace and plenty","A new temple","A returning king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:16",verse:"Go and speak to Ebed-melech the Ethiopian, saying, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will bring my words upon this city for evil, and not for good; and they shall be accomplished in that day before thee.", insight:"For evil, not for good; the city had chosen its portion, and God kept His word."},
{q:"What did God promise Ebed-melech?", options:["I will deliver thee in that day, and thou shalt not be given into the hand of the men thou fearest","Thou shalt rule in Jerusalem","Thou shalt see thy children blessed","Thou shalt die in peace"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:17",verse:"But I will deliver thee in that day, saith the LORD: and thou shalt not be given into the hand of the men of whom thou art afraid.", insight:"Deliverance for the deliverer; God pays His debts to the courageous."},
{q:"What would not happen to Ebed-melech?", options:["He would not fall by the sword","He would not see Jerusalem","He would not eat bread","He would not leave the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:18",verse:"For I will surely deliver thee, and thou shalt not fall by the sword, but thy life shall be for a prey unto thee: because thou hast put thy trust in me, saith the LORD.", insight:"Not by the sword; the man who feared God was kept from the death he feared from men."},
{q:"Why would Ebed-melech's life be given him for a prey?", options:["Because thou hast put thy trust in me, saith the LORD","Because he was a prince","Because he fled the city","Because he bribed the guard"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 39:18",verse:"For I will surely deliver thee, and thou shalt not fall by the sword, but thy life shall be for a prey unto thee: because thou hast put thy trust in me, saith the LORD.", insight:"'Because thou hast put thy trust in Me'; trust is the currency heaven honors."}
]},
{n:40, name:"The Remnant at Mizpah", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 40:9",verse:"And Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan sware unto them and to their men, saying, Fear not to serve the Chaldeans: dwell in the land, and serve the king of Babylon, and it shall be well with you."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah turned down a place of honor in Babylon and chose instead to stay in the ruined land with the poor remnant at Mizpah (Jeremiah 40:4-6)?","Did you know a Babylonian captain, Nebuzar-adan, preached Jeremiah's own message to him, saying the ruin came because the people sinned and disobeyed God (Jeremiah 40:2-3)?","Did you know the Jews scattered in Moab, Ammon, and Edom came streaming back to Mizpah when they heard a remnant had been left, and gathered a great harvest of wine and summer fruits (Jeremiah 40:11-12)?"], questions:[
{q:"Where was Jeremiah when this word came to him?", options:["At Ramah, bound in chains among the captives of Jerusalem and Judah","In the court of the prison","At Mizpah with Gedaliah","In Babylon with the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah from the LORD, after that Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard had let him go from Ramah, when he had taken him being bound in chains among all that were carried away captive of Jerusalem and Judah, which were carried away captive unto Babylon.", insight:"Bound in chains at Ramah; the prophet shared the captives' road before he was freed from it."},
{q:"Among whom had Jeremiah been bound in chains?", options:["Among all that were carried away captive of Jerusalem and Judah","Among the Babylonian soldiers","Among the priests of the temple","Among the men of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah from the LORD, after that Nebuzar-adan the captain of the guard had let him go from Ramah, when he had taken him being bound in chains among all that were carried away captive of Jerusalem and Judah, which were carried away captive unto Babylon.", insight:"He was counted with the exiles; God's messengers often walk the hard road with the people."},
{q:"What did the captain of the guard say to Jeremiah?", options:["The LORD thy God hath pronounced this evil upon this place","Thou art free to go","The king demands thy counsel","Egypt has fallen"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:2",verse:"And the captain of the guard took Jeremiah, and said unto him, The LORD thy God hath pronounced this evil upon this place.", insight:"A Babylonian captain preached Jeremiah's sermon back to him; even enemies can see God's hand."},
{q:"Why had this disaster come upon them, according to the captain?", options:["Because ye have sinned against the LORD, and have not obeyed his voice","Because the walls were weak","Because Egypt betrayed them","Because of a famine"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:3",verse:"Now the LORD hath brought it, and done according as he hath said: because ye have sinned against the LORD, and have not obeyed his voice, therefore this thing is come upon you.", insight:"Sin and disobedience, named plainly by a foreigner; sometimes outsiders see our fault clearest."},
{q:"What did Nebuzar-adan do for Jeremiah?", options:["He loosed him from the chains upon his hands","He bound him tighter","He sent him to Babylon in chains","He left him at Ramah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:4",verse:"And now, behold, I loose thee this day from the chains which were upon thine hand. If it seem good unto thee to come with me into Babylon, come; and I will look well unto thee: but if it seem ill unto thee to come with me into Babylon, forbear: behold, all the land is before thee: whither it seemeth good and convenient for thee to go, thither go.", insight:"The chains came off; after years of prison, freedom arrived through a conqueror's hand."},
{q:"What choice was Jeremiah given?", options:["Come to Babylon with honor, or forbear, for all the land was before him","Serve in the king's palace or die","Return to prison or flee","Go to Egypt or stay in chains"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:4",verse:"And now, behold, I loose thee this day from the chains which were upon thine hand. If it seem good unto thee to come with me into Babylon, come; and I will look well unto thee: but if it seem ill unto thee to come with me into Babylon, forbear: behold, all the land is before thee: whither it seemeth good and convenient for thee to go, thither go.", insight:"'All the land is before thee'; after judgment, God opened every road to His prophet."},
{q:"To whom did the captain send Jeremiah?", options:["To Gedaliah the son of Ahikam, whom the king of Babylon had made governor","To Baruch in Egypt","To the princes at Riblah","To the priests at Bethel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:5",verse:"Now while he was not yet gone back, he said, Go back also to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan, whom the king of Babylon hath made governor over the cities of Judah, and dwell with him among the people: or go wheresoever it seemeth convenient unto thee to go. So the captain of the guard gave him victuals and a reward, and let him go.", insight:"To Gedaliah the governor; God gave Jeremiah a safe harbor in the ruins."},
{q:"What did the captain of the guard give Jeremiah when he let him go?", options:["Victuals and a reward","A sword and a shield","A letter to the king","Nothing at all"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:5",verse:"Now while he was not yet gone back, he said, Go back also to Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan, whom the king of Babylon hath made governor over the cities of Judah, and dwell with him among the people: or go wheresoever it seemeth convenient unto thee to go. So the captain of the guard gave him victuals and a reward, and let him go.", insight:"Victuals and a reward from a Babylonian; God feeds His servants through unlikely hands."},
{q:"Where did Jeremiah go, and with whom did he dwell?", options:["To Gedaliah at Mizpah, among the people left in the land","To Babylon with the captives","To Egypt with the fugitives","To the wilderness alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:6",verse:"Then went Jeremiah unto Gedaliah the son of Ahikam to Mizpah; and dwelt with him among the people that were left in the land.", insight:"He chose the remnant over the empire; the prophet stayed where the broken people were."},
{q:"Who heard that Gedaliah had been made governor?", options:["The captains of the forces in the fields, and their men","The priests in Jerusalem","The elders of Babylon","The men of Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:7",verse:"Now when all the captains of the forces which were in the fields, even they and their men, heard that the king of Babylon had made Gedaliah the son of Ahikam governor in the land, and had committed unto him men, and women, and children, and of the poor of the land, of them that were not carried away captive to Babylon;", insight:"The scattered captains heard; even in ruin, news of order travels fast."},
{q:"Which captains came to Gedaliah at Mizpah?", options:["Ishmael, Johanan, Jonathan, Seraiah, the sons of Ephai, and Jezaniah","Elnathan, Delaiah, and Gemariah","Pashur and Malchiah","Jehucal and Zephaniah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:8",verse:"Then they came to Gedaliah to Mizpah, even Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and Johanan and Jonathan the sons of Kareah, and Seraiah the son of Tanhumeth, and the sons of Ephai the Netophathite, and Jezaniah the son of a Maachathite, they and their men.", insight:"A roll call of survivors; the remnant was gathering, though danger walked among them."},
{q:"What did Gedaliah swear to the captains?", options:["Fear not to serve the Chaldeans; dwell in the land, and it shall be well with you","Flee to Egypt at once","Rebuild the walls of Jerusalem","Crown a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:9",verse:"And Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan sware unto them and to their men, saying, Fear not to serve the Chaldeans: dwell in the land, and serve the king of Babylon, and it shall be well with you.", insight:"'Fear not to serve'; peace with God's appointed judgment is wisdom, not cowardice."},
{q:"What was Gedaliah's plan at Mizpah?", options:["He would dwell at Mizpah to serve the Chaldeans, while they gathered wine, summer fruits, and oil","He would raise an army","He would rebuild the temple","He would flee to Ammon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:10",verse:"As for me, behold, I will dwell at Mizpah to serve the Chaldeans, which will come unto us: but ye, gather ye wine, and summer fruits, and oil, and put them in your vessels, and dwell in your cities that ye have taken.", insight:"Wine, summer fruits, and oil; God gave the remnant a harvest in the ashes."},
{q:"For whom would Gedaliah dwell at Mizpah?", options:["To serve the Chaldeans which would come unto them","To serve the king of Egypt","To serve the priests","To serve himself"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:10",verse:"As for me, behold, I will dwell at Mizpah to serve the Chaldeans, which will come unto us: but ye, gather ye wine, and summer fruits, and oil, and put them in your vessels, and dwell in your cities that ye have taken.", insight:"He took the hard post himself; good leaders stand where the danger comes."},
{q:"Who else heard that a remnant had been left in Judah?", options:["The Jews in Moab, among the Ammonites, in Edom, and in all the countries","The men of Babylon","The Egyptians","The Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:11",verse:"Likewise when all the Jews that were in Moab, and among the Ammonites, and in Edom, and that were in all the countries, heard that the king of Babylon had left a remnant of Judah, and that he had set over them Gedaliah the son of Ahikam the son of Shaphan;", insight:"From Moab to Edom the news spread; hope travels farther than despair."},
{q:"What did the returned Jews gather at Mizpah?", options:["Wine and summer fruits very much","Gold and silver","Weapons of war","Stones for the walls"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:12",verse:"Even all the Jews returned out of all places whither they were driven, and came to the land of Judah, to Gedaliah, unto Mizpah, and gathered wine and summer fruits very much.", insight:"Wine and summer fruits, very much; even after judgment, the land gave its increase."},
{q:"What warning did Johanan bring to Gedaliah?", options:["Baalis king of the Ammonites had sent Ishmael to slay him","The Chaldeans were returning","A famine was coming","The captains were deserting"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:14",verse:"And said unto him, Dost thou certainly know that Baalis the king of the Ammonites hath sent Ishmael the son of Nethaniah to slay thee? But Gedaliah the son of Ahikam believed them not.", insight:"A true warning, plainly given; wise men weigh warnings, even from friends."},
{q:"Did Gedaliah believe the warning?", options:["No, he believed them not","Yes, and he fled","Yes, and he arrested Ishmael","He asked for proof"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:14",verse:"And said unto him, Dost thou certainly know that Baalis the king of the Ammonites hath sent Ishmael the son of Nethaniah to slay thee? But Gedaliah the son of Ahikam believed them not.", insight:"He believed them not; a trusting heart is beautiful, but not always wise."},
{q:"What did Johanan offer to do?", options:["Slay Ishmael secretly, so no man would know","Lead the people to Egypt","Challenge Ishmael openly","Warn the Chaldeans"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:15",verse:"Then Johanan the son of Kareah spake to Gedaliah in Mizpah secretly, saying, Let me go, I pray thee, and I will slay Ishmael the son of Nethaniah, and no man shall know it: wherefore should he slay thee, that all the Jews which are gathered unto thee should be scattered, and the remnant in Judah perish?", insight:"A secret killing offered as a kindness; even loyal men can propose dark solutions."},
{q:"What was Gedaliah's answer to Johanan?", options:["Thou shalt not do this thing, for thou speakest falsely of Ishmael","Do it quickly and quietly","Bring Ishmael to me","Flee with me to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 40:16",verse:"But Gedaliah the son of Ahikam said unto Johanan the son of Kareah, Thou shalt not do this thing: for thou speakest falsely of Ishmael.", insight:"'Thou speakest falsely of Ishmael'; his last recorded words defended the man who would kill him."}
]}
]);
